Ladybird's per-site User-Agent comes from its site-compatibility rules,
which a force_Ladybird transformation can point back at the browser's
own string. WebKit's GTK and WPE ports keep the same kind of table in
UserAgentQuirks::quirksForURL() (platform/glib/UserAgentQuirks.cpp),
which hands particular domains a Chrome- or macOS-shaped User-Agent
through urlRequiresChromeBrowser() and urlRequiresMacintoshPlatform().
Gecko's webcompat system add-on applies per-site User-Agent
interventions through UAHelpers in browser/extensions/webcompat/lib/
intervention_helpers.js, and nsHttpHandler.cpp honors the
general.useragent.override pref for the whole browser. Chromium keeps
no per-site table of its own; an embedder sets a per-WebContents
override through WebContents::SetUserAgentOverride(). None of the
three ships a rule that undoes a user's spoof for one site, since none
ships a spoof to undo.
